\project project_dissertation

\starttitle[title={Abstract}]
Since its inception 11 years ago, single cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-Seq) allows measurement of high-dimensional transcriptome data with single cell resolution.
This grants unprecedented opportunities for disease and development research.
This potential prompted ongoing growth in experimental capabilities, data size, and analysis tools.
Due to the this combinatorial explosion of options, arriving at a set of best practices involves both a lot of research and implementation effort.

This work describes advances towards a consolidated and accessible scRNA-Seq analysis and visualization experience.
It focuses on the development of reusable analysis building blocks and algorithms for dimensional reduction tailored to this kind of high-volume, high-dimensional data.
A general scRNA-Seq analysis workflow is explained together with the challenges and opportunities related to each step.
The author’s contributions are placed into the context of this workflow:
\n{AnnData} is a generic Python data structure for annotated matrices such as scRNA-Seq expressions.
It is used by \n{Scanpy}, an efficient Python framework with building blocks for preprocessing, analysis, and visualization of scRNA-Seq data.
\n{Destiny} is an efficient implementation of the diffusion map algorithm for R optimized for biological data.
It contains the gene relevance algorithm for detecting genes that drive a given dimensional reduction.
BART-Seq is an inexpensive protocol for targeted transcriptome quantification for large amounts of samples.

The established projects \n{AnnData}, \n{scanpy}, and \n{destiny} prove to be popular contributions to single cell transcriptome analysis.
Their advances in accessibility, performance and reproducibility improve the field, while their use by researchers and industry shows promise to power many applications up to and including clinical ones.
\stoptitle

